WEBVTT

00:00:00.273 --> 00:00:02.856
(gentle music)

00:00:11.993 --> 00:00:13.703
- Yeah, so I guess I'll get started.

00:00:13.703 --> 00:00:15.173
Hello and welcome.

00:00:15.173 --> 00:00:17.003
My name's Gregorios Kythreotis.

00:00:17.003 --> 00:00:20.453
And today I'm gonna be talking
about The Art of Sable,

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.933
Imperfection, Limitation and Worldbuilding

00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:24.033
Through Visual Design.

00:00:25.395 --> 00:00:26.228
So if you don't know, I'm
one of the co-founders

00:00:26.228 --> 00:00:30.006
of Shedworks alongside Daniel Fineberg.

00:00:30.873 --> 00:00:33.703
We started working together
in my parent's shed in 2014,

00:00:33.703 --> 00:00:36.946
and I guess just kept
kind of going from there.

00:00:37.853 --> 00:00:40.883
Development on Sable started
with just the two of us,

00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:42.673
but by the end of four
years of development,

00:00:42.673 --> 00:00:45.693
we were between six to 10
people on the core dev team

00:00:45.693 --> 00:00:47.246
at various points throughout.

00:00:48.853 --> 00:00:51.373
And before we get started
in case you don't know,

00:00:51.373 --> 00:00:55.273
Sable is a game we released
on September 23rd, 2021,

00:00:55.273 --> 00:00:57.873
and it's an open world exploration game

00:00:57.873 --> 00:01:01.063
set in a desert in which you play a Sable.

00:01:01.063 --> 00:01:03.093
And she's a young girl leaving home

00:01:03.093 --> 00:01:04.833
for the first time on her gliding.

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:06.873
And here's what it looks like.

00:01:06.873 --> 00:01:09.456
(gentle music)

00:01:16.553 --> 00:01:19.013
♪ Come in to me ♪

00:01:19.013 --> 00:01:21.542
♪ Show us the way ♪

00:01:21.542 --> 00:01:26.542
♪ I'm caught between the wind
and parts of the unknown ♪

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:32.729
♪ A door between two worlds ♪

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:34.990
♪ A sight to see ♪

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.990
♪ It feels like everything is moving ♪

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:47.260
♪ Around me ♪

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:52.260
♪ Feels like it is moving ♪

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.347
♪ In time ♪

00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:07.893
So, what is this talk about?

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:09.473
It's not a technical talk,

00:02:09.473 --> 00:02:10.953
but I will go into some details

00:02:10.953 --> 00:02:13.126
on workflow and method at points.

00:02:14.113 --> 00:02:16.693
But the focus of this
talk is really about how,

00:02:16.693 --> 00:02:18.643
as a small team, we use techniques

00:02:18.643 --> 00:02:20.373
that would allow us to create and populate

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.783
an open world game convincingly,

00:02:22.783 --> 00:02:25.213
and how we kind of turned our limitations

00:02:25.213 --> 00:02:28.596
into strengths in making the
art and the world of Sable.

00:02:30.753 --> 00:02:33.653
So the primary focus for us artistically

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.713
is always gonna be creating
something that we like,

00:02:36.713 --> 00:02:39.743
but you have to be able to
execute on that style of course,

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:42.033
and with the team and the
skillset that you have.

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:44.976
So this was our number one
focus throughout development.

00:02:45.873 --> 00:02:48.723
We really wanted to make
something that felt handcrafted

00:02:48.723 --> 00:02:50.783
and a big objective of
ours was to make the game

00:02:50.783 --> 00:02:54.463
look almost as 2D as possible
in a still or an animation,

00:02:54.463 --> 00:02:56.813
but actually be a fully
realized 3D environment

00:02:56.813 --> 00:02:58.443
that players could explore.

00:02:58.443 --> 00:03:03.113
And we also felt that if our
game had been photo realistic,

00:03:03.113 --> 00:03:04.903
people's expectations of it coming in,

00:03:04.903 --> 00:03:06.243
would've been very different.

00:03:06.243 --> 00:03:08.773
So it was good to set
expectations with the style

00:03:08.773 --> 00:03:11.856
of the game and the art that we used.

00:03:13.093 --> 00:03:16.033
So in terms of visual
references for Sable,

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:19.653
we looked a lot at 2D
animation and illustration.

00:03:19.653 --> 00:03:21.843
We talked a lot about how we looked at

00:03:21.843 --> 00:03:24.293
European clear line
style, such as Moebius,

00:03:24.293 --> 00:03:28.813
Herge and Schuiten as
important reference points.

00:03:28.813 --> 00:03:31.363
But we also looked a lot at animation.

00:03:31.363 --> 00:03:33.013
Obviously, the game is in motion.

00:03:33.013 --> 00:03:37.193
So we looked a lot at Studio
Ghibli as well as architects,

00:03:37.193 --> 00:03:42.193
such as Carlo Scarper,
Archigram, and the Metabolists.

00:03:42.873 --> 00:03:45.738
And also research trips
to Arcosanti and Marrakesh

00:03:45.738 --> 00:03:49.543
informed some of the design route we took

00:03:49.543 --> 00:03:50.773
as well as deeper research

00:03:50.773 --> 00:03:54.336
into vernacular architecture
and tent architecture.

00:03:56.213 --> 00:03:59.133
So this was a project
based around our strengths

00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:01.093
and weakness as a team.

00:04:01.093 --> 00:04:03.523
We decided to avoid doing combat because

00:04:03.523 --> 00:04:06.503
we didn't really have an
animator on the team initially,

00:04:06.503 --> 00:04:08.243
and we didn't think we
could do it justice.

00:04:08.243 --> 00:04:10.353
There are plenty of games that do combat

00:04:10.353 --> 00:04:12.263
better than we ever could.

00:04:12.263 --> 00:04:16.403
And so the project was
narrative led and art led

00:04:16.403 --> 00:04:18.313
because that's what we care about.

00:04:18.313 --> 00:04:20.566
And that's what we felt
we could execute on.

00:04:22.293 --> 00:04:24.303
Also, I have a degree in architecture,

00:04:24.303 --> 00:04:26.593
so we decided to try and
focus a lot of production

00:04:26.593 --> 00:04:28.143
and world building around that.

00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:32.963
And we wanted a style that
would be quick to produce,

00:04:32.963 --> 00:04:35.793
quick to trade on, would
be consistent visually,

00:04:35.793 --> 00:04:37.803
and would stand out.

00:04:37.803 --> 00:04:40.583
That it could be low poly was
an important part of that.

00:04:40.583 --> 00:04:43.233
And being able to use
color and consistent use

00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:45.383
of visual rules were
also really important.

00:04:46.833 --> 00:04:49.213
Our limitations informed a
lot of the world building,

00:04:49.213 --> 00:04:51.093
things like setting and culture

00:04:51.093 --> 00:04:53.486
were informed directly by our team size.

00:04:54.883 --> 00:04:56.823
For example, one of the key reasons

00:04:56.823 --> 00:04:59.353
the game is set in a
desert is because we knew

00:04:59.353 --> 00:05:00.033
we couldn't make a really
detailed open world

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:02.553
we couldn't make a really
detailed open world

00:05:02.553 --> 00:05:03.803
that was at this scale.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:07.663
The game's about 10 kilometers
by 10 kilometers in size.

00:05:07.663 --> 00:05:10.873
And, you know, the desert
probably could have been

00:05:10.873 --> 00:05:14.633
something like an ocean,
but anything more dense,

00:05:14.633 --> 00:05:17.713
it would've been much
more challenging for us.

00:05:17.713 --> 00:05:20.803
And actually we always kind
of thought of the dunes as a,

00:05:20.803 --> 00:05:22.883
almost like a seascape and the locations

00:05:22.883 --> 00:05:25.256
that players would visit
as islands of content.

00:05:27.073 --> 00:05:29.603
And this really provided
a focus for development

00:05:29.603 --> 00:05:32.313
as we could focus on specific locations

00:05:32.313 --> 00:05:35.473
in terms of density, and
allow those spaces in between

00:05:35.473 --> 00:05:37.163
to breathe a little bit.

00:05:37.163 --> 00:05:40.073
But also they act as points
of interest for the player

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:42.666
in a world that could
potentially be disorienting.

00:05:44.327 --> 00:05:46.153
This was really important
because we tried to make sure

00:05:46.153 --> 00:05:48.393
that players were focused on exploration

00:05:48.393 --> 00:05:51.143
in a kind of organic heads up sort of way,

00:05:51.143 --> 00:05:54.903
rather than relying heavily
on a minimap or map markers.

00:05:54.903 --> 00:05:57.483
So that was our kind of
prevailing philosophy

00:05:57.483 --> 00:06:00.566
as we developed the art of the game.

00:06:02.473 --> 00:06:06.273
And our primary focus was
to make a fully explorable

00:06:06.273 --> 00:06:08.613
3D open world that looked 2D.

00:06:08.613 --> 00:06:12.173
So the illusion of the game
being 2D was really important.

00:06:12.173 --> 00:06:15.443
At first, we kinda thought it
was just something fun to try.

00:06:15.443 --> 00:06:17.633
And after some exploration we found

00:06:17.633 --> 00:06:20.153
we could push it and
actually be quite successful,

00:06:20.153 --> 00:06:22.526
but this brought a whole host of problems.

00:06:23.613 --> 00:06:27.246
So one big issue with making
a flat dune shaded world,

00:06:28.183 --> 00:06:33.183
characters, and backgrounds
is reading depth in 3D space.

00:06:33.443 --> 00:06:35.873
It's already a really
big issue to overcome

00:06:35.873 --> 00:06:37.033
with 3D games in general.

00:06:37.033 --> 00:06:40.376
'Cause you are flattening a
3D environment to a 2D plane.

00:06:42.297 --> 00:06:44.673
And a lot of the time shading
and detail help with this.

00:06:44.673 --> 00:06:47.553
And obviously when you have flat shading,

00:06:47.553 --> 00:06:50.443
you are getting rid of
some of the tools you have.

00:06:50.443 --> 00:06:54.633
You can overcome some of
these with things like,

00:06:54.633 --> 00:06:58.563
in Mario Nintendo, they shine
a light down below Mario

00:06:58.563 --> 00:07:01.473
to give the player footing and guidance.

00:07:01.473 --> 00:07:04.383
It's also also the
reason why a lot of games

00:07:04.383 --> 00:07:06.183
have double jump mechanics I think,

00:07:06.183 --> 00:07:11.183
and a reason that we used this
hover mechanic in the game

00:07:12.093 --> 00:07:13.833
just to allow players to recalibrate

00:07:13.833 --> 00:07:16.163
where they sit in the environment.

00:07:16.163 --> 00:07:18.616
But we also had to solve
this on a visual basis,

00:07:19.893 --> 00:07:22.643
having committed to having
flat shade environments.

00:07:22.643 --> 00:07:25.916
So this is kind of how we
build up the rendered image.

00:07:27.413 --> 00:07:30.953
So this is the initial flat shading.

00:07:30.953 --> 00:07:33.916
No post-processing over it.

00:07:34.833 --> 00:07:39.033
And the first thing that we
add obviously is lighting.

00:07:39.033 --> 00:07:42.883
Lighting is really important
to readability and depth,

00:07:42.883 --> 00:07:45.623
having light and shadows,
just to help players

00:07:45.623 --> 00:07:48.173
figure out where they sit on a surface,

00:07:48.173 --> 00:07:51.023
where other objects sit on a
surface in relation to them.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.423
It actually was a big
motivation to adding a shadow

00:07:54.423 --> 00:07:56.333
to the moonlight in the game.

00:07:56.333 --> 00:07:59.173
We needed the player to be casting shadows

00:07:59.173 --> 00:08:01.313
as often as possible in order to make sure

00:08:01.313 --> 00:08:04.116
that they were readable on the surface.

00:08:06.003 --> 00:08:10.463
We then added this distant
fog, this distant gradient.

00:08:10.463 --> 00:08:12.673
This was really, really key

00:08:13.753 --> 00:08:15.583
to reading mid to long distances.

00:08:15.583 --> 00:08:18.953
And it was something we
actually added fairly early on

00:08:18.953 --> 00:08:21.033
and solved a bunch of problems for us,

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:23.383
definitely had the biggest impact overall.

00:08:23.383 --> 00:08:25.933
And we were able to
customize this per biome

00:08:25.933 --> 00:08:29.033
and environment to add
variety, change of tone.

00:08:29.033 --> 00:08:31.983
And it also provides the
framework for our day-night cycle.

00:08:33.903 --> 00:08:37.473
Obviously outlines and
density are kind of a key part

00:08:37.473 --> 00:08:41.583
of our style, but actually
gave us a really useful tool

00:08:41.583 --> 00:08:44.653
for keep adding depth to the world.

00:08:44.653 --> 00:08:48.883
Because they give a great
sense of perspective and scale

00:08:48.883 --> 00:08:51.683
as it kind of fades into the distance.

00:08:51.683 --> 00:08:54.293
Some environments might have
gridded lines that kind of

00:08:54.293 --> 00:08:57.253
create these perspectival focuses

00:08:57.253 --> 00:09:01.196
as well as overlapping and
underlapping in nice ways.

00:09:03.173 --> 00:09:05.583
And so we needed to have both
texturing in the environment

00:09:05.583 --> 00:09:07.753
that supports this as
well as object placement

00:09:07.753 --> 00:09:09.843
in the environment that supported this.

00:09:09.843 --> 00:09:11.613
And again creates some more readable

00:09:11.613 --> 00:09:13.106
3D environment for players.

00:09:15.213 --> 00:09:16.713
And an added detail to this

00:09:16.713 --> 00:09:19.583
is the fading opacity on outlines.

00:09:19.583 --> 00:09:24.273
So as outlines go into the
distance, we fade them out.

00:09:24.273 --> 00:09:27.043
This again, emphasizes
the perspectival lines,

00:09:27.043 --> 00:09:32.043
but also allows us to create
some really nice fog effects

00:09:32.163 --> 00:09:34.333
in the game, because we have a game

00:09:34.333 --> 00:09:36.243
where we're rendering
stuff quite far away,

00:09:36.243 --> 00:09:37.663
and we need that to work.

00:09:37.663 --> 00:09:40.113
And the player is traveling
quite long distances.

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:45.353
This kind of helps us disguise
objects as they fade in.

00:09:45.353 --> 00:09:46.673
At some point we didn't have this

00:09:46.673 --> 00:09:48.093
and objects would just kind of pop in

00:09:48.093 --> 00:09:51.396
and it was kind of ugly,
and a real problem for us.

00:09:52.253 --> 00:09:55.103
And actually this effect
solved a bunch of that

00:09:55.103 --> 00:09:57.393
whilst also actually
making things more readable

00:09:57.393 --> 00:09:59.043
in the foreground on top of that.

00:10:05.693 --> 00:10:07.823
It was really important to style.

00:10:07.823 --> 00:10:09.023
It's one of the more recognizable

00:10:09.023 --> 00:10:10.843
visual components of the game.

00:10:10.843 --> 00:10:14.003
And it communicates mood and
tone of a lot of locations,

00:10:14.003 --> 00:10:16.303
as well as providing variety and setting

00:10:16.303 --> 00:10:19.323
that could easily feel
one tone and monotonous.

00:10:19.323 --> 00:10:22.223
It's a desert, you know,
how much variety do you get?

00:10:22.223 --> 00:10:25.503
Actually quite a lot, but
your initial perception

00:10:25.503 --> 00:10:27.596
would be that it's pretty one tone.

00:10:28.603 --> 00:10:31.133
And it let us do things
like day-night cycles.

00:10:31.133 --> 00:10:33.233
Here's an example of
schemes that we can set

00:10:33.233 --> 00:10:37.626
in the same environment using
color and color profiles.

00:10:38.663 --> 00:10:41.576
And here's how those schemes
transition in a time lapse.

00:10:42.713 --> 00:10:46.573
So you get a really nice
sense of the tone shifting

00:10:46.573 --> 00:10:48.836
as you spend time in the same place.

00:10:51.593 --> 00:10:53.823
So we use these things
called color profiles

00:10:53.823 --> 00:10:55.543
to set different attributes

00:10:55.543 --> 00:10:58.220
for various times of day and biomes.

00:10:59.613 --> 00:11:00.913
So as an artist, I could go in

00:11:00.913 --> 00:11:04.243
and tweak various values
in the distance gradient

00:11:04.243 --> 00:11:08.773
as well as set shadow intensity
where lines start and end,

00:11:08.773 --> 00:11:10.803
and where gradient starts and ends.

00:11:10.803 --> 00:11:14.443
So actually this provided
incredible framework

00:11:14.443 --> 00:11:18.406
for being able to create
diversity across the game.

00:11:19.693 --> 00:11:21.883
And these gradients, the gradients

00:11:21.883 --> 00:11:24.153
that I mentioned earlier
applied over distance.

00:11:24.153 --> 00:11:25.863
And we can tweak this per biome,

00:11:25.863 --> 00:11:29.433
and even adjust this when
players move indoors or outdoors.

00:11:29.433 --> 00:11:32.563
So when what the player
needs to read shortens,

00:11:32.563 --> 00:11:34.133
we can actually bring the gradients in

00:11:34.133 --> 00:11:36.663
and create a more
dramatic looking gradient

00:11:36.663 --> 00:11:39.203
that looks kind of awkward outside,

00:11:39.203 --> 00:11:41.056
but inside looks really effective.

00:11:42.413 --> 00:11:44.523
And it's just a tool that
gave us a lot of control

00:11:44.523 --> 00:11:46.166
and consistency in the style.

00:11:47.983 --> 00:11:50.053
And yeah, we spent a lot of
time thinking about colors

00:11:50.053 --> 00:11:51.593
and different color schemes

00:11:51.593 --> 00:11:54.663
acted almost like rewards for exploration.

00:11:54.663 --> 00:11:56.773
Transitions between
colors also communicate

00:11:56.773 --> 00:11:58.923
a sense of journey and
the passage of time.

00:12:00.503 --> 00:12:01.883
And it also meant that we could have

00:12:01.883 --> 00:12:04.333
elements repeating in the environment,

00:12:04.333 --> 00:12:06.713
but feel recontextualized
in different locations

00:12:06.713 --> 00:12:08.063
at different times of day.

00:12:08.063 --> 00:12:10.863
These kind of intentionally
similar structures

00:12:11.813 --> 00:12:13.373
in terms of the world building

00:12:13.373 --> 00:12:17.183
just made them feel less mechanical

00:12:19.873 --> 00:12:20.706
Outlines.

00:12:20.706 --> 00:12:22.513
Again, I'm gonna go back to those

00:12:22.513 --> 00:12:23.843
because they're a huge part of the style.

00:12:23.843 --> 00:12:27.466
And I'll go back to how
we kind of generated them.

00:12:28.333 --> 00:12:29.723
There's a lot of detail to add here,

00:12:29.723 --> 00:12:31.843
and I'm actually not the best
person to ask about this,

00:12:31.843 --> 00:12:36.163
but I'll go over the three
main ways we create linework.

00:12:36.163 --> 00:12:39.423
So yeah, we have three techniques.

00:12:39.423 --> 00:12:42.943
The first one is a fairly
standard automatic edge detection

00:12:42.943 --> 00:12:45.003
for drawing lines based on distances.

00:12:45.003 --> 00:12:48.783
This is something that we
could tweak on a shade basis.

00:12:48.783 --> 00:12:53.363
So, and you can see it really
effectively in the dunes,

00:12:53.363 --> 00:12:56.863
where the lines kind of end nicely.

00:12:56.863 --> 00:13:00.033
And yeah, it was a really useful tool

00:13:00.033 --> 00:13:01.826
and it was just automatic.

00:13:03.903 --> 00:13:05.263
The second way of drawing lines

00:13:05.263 --> 00:13:07.693
is probably the most important
to the style that we have.

00:13:07.693 --> 00:13:09.243
And this is vertex painting.

00:13:09.243 --> 00:13:12.973
So we vertex paint objects that
we want lines to be around.

00:13:12.973 --> 00:13:16.173
This method is extremely
stable and reliable

00:13:16.173 --> 00:13:20.363
and it creates this extremely
consistent linework,

00:13:20.363 --> 00:13:23.483
but it requires a lot of
manual work as an artist,

00:13:23.483 --> 00:13:25.283
but also gives you a lot of control.

00:13:26.763 --> 00:13:28.283
And you can get issues where if you have

00:13:28.283 --> 00:13:30.823
too many vertex colors
and too many objects,

00:13:30.823 --> 00:13:33.552
especially over distance where linework

00:13:33.552 --> 00:13:35.733
gets really kind of mushed together

00:13:35.733 --> 00:13:37.953
and you just get these intense,

00:13:37.953 --> 00:13:40.563
like dithering black
blobs in the distance.

00:13:40.563 --> 00:13:43.553
So it takes a bit of fiddling to get used

00:13:43.553 --> 00:13:44.903
to what works for the game,

00:13:44.903 --> 00:13:48.123
but you just kind of develop that sense

00:13:48.123 --> 00:13:50.483
as you work with it more.

00:13:50.483 --> 00:13:52.683
And the final technique
we use is something

00:13:52.683 --> 00:13:54.236
we call noise texture.

00:13:55.833 --> 00:13:57.303
So this is a black and white texture

00:13:57.303 --> 00:14:00.433
that we made a specific
slot for on the shader.

00:14:00.433 --> 00:14:02.793
And we draw lines between the thresholds

00:14:02.793 --> 00:14:04.116
of black and white here.

00:14:05.793 --> 00:14:08.783
And this let us introduce...

00:14:08.783 --> 00:14:10.933
Well, it is just like a standard texture,

00:14:10.933 --> 00:14:13.703
but we could draw outlines
around it basically.

00:14:13.703 --> 00:14:17.333
But because we could separate
the color and the noise,

00:14:17.333 --> 00:14:19.913
it meant that actually we
could have differentiate

00:14:19.913 --> 00:14:22.443
the two textures and
create some interesting

00:14:22.443 --> 00:14:24.606
kind of patterns there.

00:14:25.983 --> 00:14:29.363
So, these raw techniques
that really focused

00:14:29.363 --> 00:14:32.913
on our limitations as a
team, low poly models,

00:14:32.913 --> 00:14:35.163
bold use of color and color profiles

00:14:35.163 --> 00:14:38.173
meant that we could iterate on art

00:14:38.173 --> 00:14:41.276
in a kind of broad way very quickly.

00:14:42.593 --> 00:14:44.643
And this was really crucial for us

00:14:44.643 --> 00:14:47.246
to create a coherent and consistent style.

00:14:49.333 --> 00:14:51.953
Another one of the things that we tried

00:14:51.953 --> 00:14:55.673
to introduce in the game was imperfection.

00:14:55.673 --> 00:14:58.503
So one of the dangers
with working with outlines

00:14:58.503 --> 00:15:00.033
is that I think it could potentially

00:15:04.373 --> 00:15:09.373
And like a really perfectly
made computer generated project.

00:15:10.993 --> 00:15:12.863
Computers are quite good
at doing perfect things.

00:15:12.863 --> 00:15:14.253
And generally we wanna introduce

00:15:14.253 --> 00:15:16.343
some element of imperfection to the art.

00:15:16.343 --> 00:15:18.973
So this was how we tried to do it.

00:15:18.973 --> 00:15:22.013
First and foremost, and
most controversially,

00:15:22.013 --> 00:15:25.493
we added limited frame animations
for character animation

00:15:25.493 --> 00:15:27.066
and for creature animations.

00:15:28.483 --> 00:15:30.773
We don't tend to do this
for environmental elements

00:15:30.773 --> 00:15:32.293
or anything affected by physics,

00:15:32.293 --> 00:15:34.813
but this was just to emphasize and push

00:15:34.813 --> 00:15:38.146
that 2D and 3D feeling.

00:15:39.933 --> 00:15:41.123
Another one of the things that we did

00:15:41.123 --> 00:15:43.983
was to kind of wobble our
geometry lines where we could.

00:15:43.983 --> 00:15:46.493
So instead of these
perfectly straight lines,

00:15:46.493 --> 00:15:48.563
we just add a little bit of imperfection.

00:15:48.563 --> 00:15:50.513
We tried to do that everywhere we could

00:15:51.433 --> 00:15:54.233
just to disrupt that pure straight line.

00:15:54.233 --> 00:15:56.753
And this just helps to make the game

00:15:56.753 --> 00:15:59.056
feel more handcrafted
and handmade I think.

00:16:01.173 --> 00:16:03.003
And going back to the textures,

00:16:03.003 --> 00:16:07.603
all of our textures were drawn
in Procreate and hand drawn,

00:16:07.603 --> 00:16:10.213
and try to keep them quite loose

00:16:10.213 --> 00:16:12.543
and leave imperfections in there,

00:16:12.543 --> 00:16:16.563
just to again, make it
feel handmade and leave

00:16:16.563 --> 00:16:20.403
that kind of thumbprint of
the creator in the project.

00:16:20.403 --> 00:16:22.493
And this also extends to
some of our hand drawn

00:16:22.493 --> 00:16:25.543
VFX animations, also drawn in Procreate

00:16:25.543 --> 00:16:27.036
and brought into Unity.

00:16:28.783 --> 00:16:31.183
And I think this just brings that 2D look

00:16:31.183 --> 00:16:33.453
in the motion parts of the game.

00:16:33.453 --> 00:16:36.423
And it gives the game a lot of character,

00:16:36.423 --> 00:16:40.286
as well as giving myself a
lot of control as an artist.

00:16:41.353 --> 00:16:46.353
One thing that we did
automate though was rocks.

00:16:46.623 --> 00:16:48.003
There's a lot of rocks in our game,

00:16:48.003 --> 00:16:50.163
and we need to be able
to reshape them quickly,

00:16:50.163 --> 00:16:54.133
and reuse them in a variety of ways.

00:16:54.133 --> 00:16:58.693
So we had this noise
based shader that let us

00:16:58.693 --> 00:17:01.273
basically kind of copy and
paste and move around rocks

00:17:01.273 --> 00:17:03.776
really quickly for level design purposes.

00:17:04.894 --> 00:17:09.894
But keep a consistent
scale and visual style.

00:17:10.333 --> 00:17:13.163
This was a incredibly
important tool that came

00:17:13.163 --> 00:17:16.516
actually pretty late, but
gave us a lot of power.

00:17:20.163 --> 00:17:23.453
Yeah, so embracing these
visual imperfections

00:17:23.453 --> 00:17:25.453
was something we felt was really crucial

00:17:25.453 --> 00:17:27.523
for the style of the game.

00:17:27.523 --> 00:17:30.133
But the main goal was just
to create this layered image

00:17:30.133 --> 00:17:34.603
that felt coherent and kind
of captivating as a whole.

00:17:34.603 --> 00:17:38.606
And I think all those different
elements tied into that.

00:17:41.023 --> 00:17:44.243
So another technique
we used was modularity.

00:17:44.243 --> 00:17:47.753
We used this in masks, NPCs and bikes.

00:17:47.753 --> 00:17:51.103
So from very early on,
we decided that masks

00:17:51.103 --> 00:17:53.043
were a really important
part of the visual design,

00:17:53.043 --> 00:17:54.946
but also the world building of Sable.

00:17:56.912 --> 00:17:58.703
This was because we knew we were gonna try

00:17:58.703 --> 00:18:03.703
and make a larger world,
but I'm not an animator.

00:18:03.813 --> 00:18:06.143
I was afraid of animating faces.

00:18:06.143 --> 00:18:08.373
So we just decided to do masks.

00:18:08.373 --> 00:18:13.093
And this, you know, then became
a really primary narrative

00:18:13.093 --> 00:18:14.253
and art focus of the game.

00:18:14.253 --> 00:18:17.096
So we kind of embraced and
lean into that limitation.

00:18:19.593 --> 00:18:22.083
And I think keeping
those points of friction

00:18:22.083 --> 00:18:26.083
and working with them is where
good design tends to occur.

00:18:26.083 --> 00:18:28.303
And I think that really
worked for us here.

00:18:28.303 --> 00:18:30.846
And in terms how those designs came about,

00:18:31.743 --> 00:18:34.183
kind of just take 'em
on a case by case basis,

00:18:34.183 --> 00:18:37.273
there weren't any specific
cultural or historical examples

00:18:37.273 --> 00:18:39.753
that we focused on, but we focused instead

00:18:39.753 --> 00:18:43.183
more on in world symbolism, communication,

00:18:43.183 --> 00:18:45.436
functionality and materiality.

00:18:47.833 --> 00:18:51.913
And this modularity went into
our character customization.

00:18:51.913 --> 00:18:55.103
So all our clothing and
characters are built modularly.

00:18:55.103 --> 00:18:58.006
Again, because we knew we had
to populate a larger world.

00:18:59.093 --> 00:19:02.103
And this was a really
early decision we made

00:19:02.103 --> 00:19:04.643
when it was just a two person team even,

00:19:04.643 --> 00:19:07.593
and became one of the
core systems in terms of

00:19:09.693 --> 00:19:13.403
the player being able to
customize Sable herself.

00:19:13.403 --> 00:19:18.403
But it also gave us a really
nice amount of variety,

00:19:18.433 --> 00:19:21.483
not just in visual language,
in terms of clothing,

00:19:21.483 --> 00:19:24.643
and masks, but also I
think we get some fun,

00:19:24.643 --> 00:19:28.453
like variety in body
shapes and silhouettes,

00:19:28.453 --> 00:19:30.253
which is really important in a world

00:19:30.253 --> 00:19:32.483
where you can't actually
see anyone's face.

00:19:32.483 --> 00:19:34.193
But we still want the player to connect

00:19:34.193 --> 00:19:38.166
with these characters and
remember who they are.

00:19:40.593 --> 00:19:43.163
And then turning that into a system

00:19:43.163 --> 00:19:44.963
for the player to access meant that

00:19:44.963 --> 00:19:48.043
we then had a tool for rewarding players.

00:19:48.043 --> 00:19:51.573
So we could reward players
with masks, clothing items,

00:19:51.573 --> 00:19:55.156
and that then feeds into the
exploration cycle of the game.

00:19:56.513 --> 00:19:59.276
And then another system
was bike modularity.

00:20:00.183 --> 00:20:03.693
Again, really important for
populating a large world

00:20:03.693 --> 00:20:07.463
with such a small team,
but also another system

00:20:07.463 --> 00:20:08.473
for player expression.

00:20:08.473 --> 00:20:11.043
So we made it so that you could

00:20:11.043 --> 00:20:12.623
customize components on your bike

00:20:12.623 --> 00:20:14.883
and that would affect
the physics of the bike,

00:20:14.883 --> 00:20:19.533
the audio of the bike, but
also you could change color

00:20:19.533 --> 00:20:21.333
for self-expression reasons.

00:20:21.333 --> 00:20:24.623
And you get some really nice variety

00:20:24.623 --> 00:20:26.273
and variation with those systems.

00:20:27.763 --> 00:20:30.553
So all of these modular
elements allowed us

00:20:30.553 --> 00:20:33.303
to scale up and create
variation across the world,

00:20:33.303 --> 00:20:35.673
but also allowed us to embed storytelling

00:20:35.673 --> 00:20:38.993
and well building elements
into the art of each component.

00:20:38.993 --> 00:20:41.063
Once those systems were established,

00:20:41.063 --> 00:20:43.093
they could be turned
into systems for players

00:20:43.093 --> 00:20:46.263
to interact with, which
then added significant value

00:20:46.263 --> 00:20:48.416
in terms of being able to reward players.

00:20:49.533 --> 00:20:52.473
And that was extremely
important in a world

00:20:52.473 --> 00:20:54.832
where exploration was
key, but we didn't have

00:20:54.832 --> 00:20:56.763
these kind of more circular combat,

00:20:56.763 --> 00:20:58.896
or survival systems to lean on.

00:21:01.683 --> 00:21:05.513
Yeah, and then, so an
obvious key system in Sable

00:21:05.513 --> 00:21:08.333
are the landscapes and biomes.

00:21:08.333 --> 00:21:10.323
So these were really important

00:21:10.323 --> 00:21:11.653
to the exploration of the game.

00:21:11.653 --> 00:21:15.073
We tried to think about
nature in this world

00:21:15.073 --> 00:21:16.743
in a slightly less constructed way

00:21:16.743 --> 00:21:18.973
than we did with the architecture.

00:21:18.973 --> 00:21:21.263
So we used a procedural
tool called Map Magic

00:21:21.263 --> 00:21:24.173
to create these broad stroke patterns

00:21:24.173 --> 00:21:27.533
for six biomes each with our own

00:21:27.533 --> 00:21:29.926
design language tone and visual rhythm.

00:21:32.453 --> 00:21:35.803
And we tried to use as a
natural environment generated

00:21:36.863 --> 00:21:41.143
as a kind of context for the other levels

00:21:41.143 --> 00:21:43.263
or environments that we developed.

00:21:43.263 --> 00:21:45.313
And it helped us kind of reduce the burden

00:21:45.313 --> 00:21:47.393
on decision making and design

00:21:47.393 --> 00:21:49.546
when generating those environments.

00:21:50.713 --> 00:21:53.943
Here's an example of what one
of the biome generation nodes

00:21:53.943 --> 00:21:55.686
looks like within Map Magic.

00:21:57.603 --> 00:22:01.373
And here's a breakdown of how
those biomes are built up.

00:22:01.373 --> 00:22:05.163
So, we generally have this
kind of top level dune,

00:22:05.163 --> 00:22:09.843
and this is sort of like
waves on the top of...

00:22:09.843 --> 00:22:11.306
Like sandy waves on top.

00:22:12.233 --> 00:22:14.633
And then we have a second layer underneath

00:22:14.633 --> 00:22:17.053
which acts as a kind of underlying rocks,

00:22:17.053 --> 00:22:21.773
and provides a kind of broader
directionality to an area,

00:22:21.773 --> 00:22:24.883
and lends, gives a player a tool

00:22:24.883 --> 00:22:27.773
for kind of sensing where
they are in the world.

00:22:27.773 --> 00:22:29.613
Like they feel like they're
going up a hill or down a hill

00:22:29.613 --> 00:22:32.443
rather than just across dunes.

00:22:32.443 --> 00:22:35.393
And also creates more dynamic
and interesting environments.

00:22:36.343 --> 00:22:39.203
And then we use modifiers
on the top dune layer

00:22:39.203 --> 00:22:41.573
to give each biome more
of a kind of rhythm

00:22:41.573 --> 00:22:43.333
and distinct feeling.

00:22:43.333 --> 00:22:46.063
In this example, we have
these kind of clay mounds,

00:22:46.063 --> 00:22:47.173
but in other biomes they might be

00:22:47.173 --> 00:22:49.626
cliffs or other disruptions.

00:22:51.873 --> 00:22:54.073
And then we have these smaller rocks,

00:22:54.073 --> 00:22:58.563
which each biome has kind of
unique distribution patterns,

00:22:58.563 --> 00:23:02.953
and silhouettes, and
spacing, and scale on these.

00:23:02.953 --> 00:23:06.133
They give a lot of color and
personality to each biome.

00:23:06.133 --> 00:23:08.953
And the spacing can kind of relate

00:23:08.953 --> 00:23:10.733
to other elements of the biome.

00:23:10.733 --> 00:23:14.023
So in this example, I
think certain types of rock

00:23:14.023 --> 00:23:15.936
only gather around the clay mounds.

00:23:17.493 --> 00:23:19.223
And this is also true for the foliage.

00:23:19.223 --> 00:23:23.353
So the foliage adds a real sense of life

00:23:23.353 --> 00:23:25.503
and movement to the
biome as well as color.

00:23:26.803 --> 00:23:29.433
But again, if you get the
patterns of distribution right,

00:23:29.433 --> 00:23:32.543
they can lend a real
sense of believability.

00:23:32.543 --> 00:23:33.873
If you have a certain kind of plan

00:23:33.873 --> 00:23:35.573
only growing around a certain thing,

00:23:35.573 --> 00:23:39.946
then it looks like a kind of
naturally formed environment.

00:23:42.038 --> 00:23:43.743
And then we have wildlife,
which we actually

00:23:43.743 --> 00:23:45.076
mostly place by hand.

00:23:46.351 --> 00:23:49.143
We focus these around players,

00:23:49.143 --> 00:23:51.463
around locations that
the player will explore

00:23:52.433 --> 00:23:54.493
because they can kind of act as guidance

00:23:54.493 --> 00:23:56.073
or things that will attract the player eye

00:23:56.073 --> 00:23:57.426
as they explore this world.

00:23:59.213 --> 00:24:02.283
And what wildlife went where
was related to the biome

00:24:02.283 --> 00:24:04.113
that they were placed into.

00:24:04.113 --> 00:24:06.893
And this was in order to further emphasize

00:24:06.893 --> 00:24:09.163
geographic differences.

00:24:09.163 --> 00:24:12.163
So we had different birds, beetles,

00:24:12.163 --> 00:24:14.156
and butterflies for each area.

00:24:15.963 --> 00:24:20.323
Color profiles added a
lot of variety to biomes.

00:24:20.323 --> 00:24:21.563
And then of course audio,

00:24:21.563 --> 00:24:25.033
each biome has unique music
and sound effect behavior

00:24:25.033 --> 00:24:27.733
adding to different tonal
texture throughout the game.

00:24:30.803 --> 00:24:32.683
And yeah, here are a couple of examples

00:24:32.683 --> 00:24:35.933
for different biome
slices that we generate

00:24:35.933 --> 00:24:37.073
throughout the game.

00:24:37.073 --> 00:24:39.023
You can see how we use
different noise patterns

00:24:39.023 --> 00:24:40.933
to create variation.

00:24:40.933 --> 00:24:44.506
Each of these slices is about
500 meters by 500 meters.

00:24:47.613 --> 00:24:49.790
And the final thing we
did within these biomes

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:53.533
was to kind of add these
patches that would add

00:24:53.533 --> 00:24:56.773
variation or unique
characteristics to the biomes.

00:24:56.773 --> 00:24:59.793
These gave players variation of location,

00:24:59.793 --> 00:25:00.033
but also gave us an additional landmark

00:25:07.383 --> 00:25:09.383
how you break your rules is important

00:25:09.383 --> 00:25:10.706
as the rules that you set.

00:25:12.593 --> 00:25:14.433
And the nice thing about
the wellbeing procedure

00:25:14.433 --> 00:25:15.863
was that actually creating these

00:25:15.863 --> 00:25:18.013
and adding them was a
relatively quick process

00:25:18.013 --> 00:25:19.996
once we'd established the workflow.

00:25:21.503 --> 00:25:24.523
And just to touch on
quickly, the dunes themselves

00:25:24.523 --> 00:25:26.393
being a really important
part of the visual

00:25:26.393 --> 00:25:28.343
and design language of Sable.

00:25:28.343 --> 00:25:30.563
They kind of act as
this natural obfuscation

00:25:30.563 --> 00:25:31.663
built into the environment.

00:25:31.663 --> 00:25:34.183
So as the player explores the world,

00:25:34.183 --> 00:25:35.553
they might see a point of interest,

00:25:35.553 --> 00:25:37.003
decide that they wanna go over to it

00:25:37.003 --> 00:25:40.313
and on their journey to that place,

00:25:40.313 --> 00:25:42.973
it disappears out of view
and then reappears later on,

00:25:42.973 --> 00:25:45.373
or they find something else,
or they get a bit lost.

00:25:45.373 --> 00:25:46.603
And that was a really important part

00:25:46.603 --> 00:25:48.656
of the design of the world.

00:25:51.183 --> 00:25:52.506
And, yeah, again, I just want to show

00:25:52.506 --> 00:25:57.506
an example of the different
color profiles and biomes.

00:25:57.681 --> 00:26:02.193
And finally, the architectural
language of the game

00:26:02.193 --> 00:26:04.543
is something that we
developed a bit over time.

00:26:06.263 --> 00:26:08.453
We thought of it in three kind of strata.

00:26:08.453 --> 00:26:12.553
So we had atomic,
monumental, and nomadic eras.

00:26:12.553 --> 00:26:14.363
This was a kind of
reflection of the journey

00:26:14.363 --> 00:26:17.296
that humans took when they
arrived on this planet.

00:26:18.173 --> 00:26:20.033
And we needed each element to reflect

00:26:20.033 --> 00:26:22.443
their evolving culture,
the evolving technology

00:26:22.443 --> 00:26:24.043
and materials available to them.

00:26:25.723 --> 00:26:28.923
We have this atomic,
this kind of Sci-Fi era,

00:26:28.923 --> 00:26:31.393
which relates to this
ship called the Whale

00:26:31.393 --> 00:26:36.393
and all of these come from
this one ship in the world.

00:26:36.523 --> 00:26:39.723
And that humans living on the planet

00:26:39.723 --> 00:26:43.033
have kind of looted and
relied upon for generations.

00:26:43.033 --> 00:26:47.773
And yeah, we try to maintain
a consistent style there.

00:26:47.773 --> 00:26:49.623
We have the monumental era.

00:26:49.623 --> 00:26:51.073
This comes from an era where humans

00:26:51.073 --> 00:26:53.673
have kind of tried to
tame the land a little bit

00:26:53.673 --> 00:26:56.773
and ties into some of the more unique,

00:26:56.773 --> 00:27:00.023
contextual elements of
the planet related to like

00:27:00.023 --> 00:27:03.593
the hoveral mobility, which
we call the perpetual.

00:27:03.593 --> 00:27:05.473
And these structures tend to be more tied

00:27:05.473 --> 00:27:08.173
into religious practice,
or historically had

00:27:08.173 --> 00:27:10.003
functional ideas behind them.

00:27:10.003 --> 00:27:12.533
Like observatory, step wells,

00:27:12.533 --> 00:27:16.263
old villages, or scouting towers.

00:27:16.263 --> 00:27:20.056
The idea is that they try to
use utilize rock and material.

00:27:21.793 --> 00:27:23.803
And then finally we have this nomadic era,

00:27:23.803 --> 00:27:27.573
which is the kind of
modern era of this world.

00:27:27.573 --> 00:27:30.143
And generally speaking,
people are living in tents

00:27:30.143 --> 00:27:31.976
or in more fabric architectures.

00:27:32.883 --> 00:27:37.883
And this is people who have
generally adapted better

00:27:38.203 --> 00:27:40.553
to the conditions of this
planet and kind of...

00:27:42.323 --> 00:27:45.966
Yeah, that was the basis
of that architecture.

00:27:47.018 --> 00:27:49.230
Each of these eras have their own design

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:50.473
and visual languages.

00:27:50.473 --> 00:27:53.503
And we use color profiles
and modular materials

00:27:53.503 --> 00:27:55.273
and props to tie things together.

00:27:55.273 --> 00:27:57.733
But we could also overlap these things

00:27:57.733 --> 00:28:02.733
and show them interacting
to show points of tension

00:28:02.943 --> 00:28:06.016
between them, but also
create interesting variation.

00:28:06.943 --> 00:28:08.593
One example is the town of Eccria.

00:28:08.593 --> 00:28:11.673
This started off as a monumental system

00:28:11.673 --> 00:28:13.383
and then was converted into a town

00:28:13.383 --> 00:28:16.573
that's more suited to desert living.

00:28:16.573 --> 00:28:18.763
And, you know, it has like outer walls

00:28:18.763 --> 00:28:22.473
to protect people from sandstorms,

00:28:22.473 --> 00:28:25.093
embraces technological solutions
to heating and cooling,

00:28:25.093 --> 00:28:28.143
but that are more passive.

00:28:28.143 --> 00:28:29.403
And this was the sort of stuff

00:28:29.403 --> 00:28:33.426
that was inspired by our trips
to Arcosanti, for example.

00:28:37.051 --> 00:28:38.753
And there are also moments
where nature or ruin

00:28:38.753 --> 00:28:40.853
have kind of overtaken the old structures.

00:28:40.853 --> 00:28:44.393
And I think it's really important
to show this interaction,

00:28:44.393 --> 00:28:46.456
to give the world of lived in feeling.

00:28:48.193 --> 00:28:50.963
And in terms of workflow for
develop developing these areas,

00:28:50.963 --> 00:28:53.903
we tend to go for a few key stages.

00:28:53.903 --> 00:28:55.993
So first was the site placement.

00:28:55.993 --> 00:28:57.923
Like I said before, we generate the biomes

00:28:57.923 --> 00:28:59.373
and we find ourselves a site.

00:29:00.643 --> 00:29:04.113
This is something that I really wanted

00:29:04.113 --> 00:29:06.523
from my background in architecture.

00:29:06.523 --> 00:29:09.743
Something that you rely
on a lot as an architect.

00:29:09.743 --> 00:29:13.936
So we just accepted what we
were given there basically.

00:29:15.143 --> 00:29:16.603
And once we'd found a suitable site,

00:29:16.603 --> 00:29:19.303
we developed white box
scenes using Probuilder.

00:29:19.303 --> 00:29:21.803
This was a really fantastic tool for us

00:29:21.803 --> 00:29:23.613
for being able to test spacing

00:29:23.613 --> 00:29:25.833
and to iterate levels quickly.

00:29:25.833 --> 00:29:27.753
We could also actually apply materials

00:29:27.753 --> 00:29:32.373
and paint vertices for our structures.

00:29:32.373 --> 00:29:34.236
And it got us really far actually.

00:29:35.563 --> 00:29:37.473
And then there were prefabs.

00:29:37.473 --> 00:29:39.993
So we would bring various related prefabs

00:29:39.993 --> 00:29:43.203
that we'd created to help theme levels,

00:29:43.203 --> 00:29:45.783
as well as provide easy
ways to get generic logic

00:29:45.783 --> 00:29:49.016
like doors, and switches into levels.

00:29:50.153 --> 00:29:52.413
And whereas we could keep things generally

00:29:52.413 --> 00:29:53.703
thematically consistent.

00:29:53.703 --> 00:29:57.393
Mixing these up would also
provide interesting opportunities

00:29:57.393 --> 00:29:59.643
for storytelling,
environmental storytelling.

00:30:07.383 --> 00:30:10.423
for creating something
that fit more neatly

00:30:10.423 --> 00:30:13.216
into a specific environment
or level geometry.

00:30:15.773 --> 00:30:17.073
So, yeah, we realized early on

00:30:17.073 --> 00:30:19.183
that we could never
really handcraft a world

00:30:19.183 --> 00:30:22.043
the size that we wanted,
with such a small team team

00:30:22.043 --> 00:30:23.523
and be able to iterate design.

00:30:23.523 --> 00:30:26.453
So we used all of these factors to inform

00:30:26.453 --> 00:30:29.573
our world building, to
inform the art of the game.

00:30:29.573 --> 00:30:33.203
And we tried to embrace some
of the unpredictable nature

00:30:33.203 --> 00:30:37.673
of procedural generations,
both in biome generation,

00:30:37.673 --> 00:30:40.453
but also in how we placed and laid out

00:30:40.453 --> 00:30:42.226
handcrafted locations to the game.

00:30:43.427 --> 00:30:46.027
And yeah, I think it ended
up serving us quite well.

00:30:47.163 --> 00:30:48.613
We're happy with it at least.

00:30:49.633 --> 00:30:50.843
So in terms of takeaways.

00:30:50.843 --> 00:30:54.323
The things that I would
say most people could learn

00:30:54.323 --> 00:30:57.493
is that you should make a
project based on the assessments

00:30:57.493 --> 00:30:59.983
of your team strengths and weaknesses,

00:30:59.983 --> 00:31:03.056
and try to plan and
build a team around that.

00:31:04.223 --> 00:31:05.503
To lean into your weaknesses

00:31:05.503 --> 00:31:06.953
in your world building if you can.

00:31:06.953 --> 00:31:08.983
And I think you can come up with some

00:31:08.983 --> 00:31:10.836
really interesting results there.

00:31:11.983 --> 00:31:14.353
Yeah, that was a big thing for us.

00:31:14.353 --> 00:31:17.133
Modularity as a tool for populating

00:31:17.133 --> 00:31:18.843
in planet like systems basically

00:31:18.843 --> 00:31:21.053
for populating things ahead of time.

00:31:21.053 --> 00:31:24.683
Rather than just jumping in,
thinking about how you can

00:31:24.683 --> 00:31:28.166
kind of help systemize your
design is really helpful.

00:31:29.423 --> 00:31:30.953
Embracing imperfection.

00:31:30.953 --> 00:31:33.913
I think it can be really
helpful for adding

00:31:33.913 --> 00:31:34.746
a kind of thumbprint.

00:31:34.746 --> 00:31:37.383
But also when you're kind of
scrambling to get stuff in

00:31:37.383 --> 00:31:41.716
later on, maybe a level of
imperfection will look okay.

00:31:43.213 --> 00:31:45.546
And yeah, just focusing on readability.

00:31:46.563 --> 00:31:49.263
I think that's just a
paramount thing that, you know,

00:31:49.263 --> 00:31:50.303
you could make a game look good.

00:31:50.303 --> 00:31:53.316
But if it's not playable,
then you're gonna struggle.

00:31:54.343 --> 00:31:56.215
And thank you for watching.

00:31:56.215 --> 00:31:58.465
I hope you have a lovely GDC, thanks.

00:31:58.465 --> 00:32:01.465
(audience clapping)

00:32:02.702 --> 00:32:05.285
(gentle music)
